 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Change delivery via self-hosted WebSockets.** Updates are pushed to browsers over self-hosted WebSocket connections rather than HTTPS polling or a third-party hosted realtime service. HTTPS polling (5-second cadence) was rejected due to latency and high redundant read load across 800 clients. Hosted realtime services (e.g., Pusher, Ably) were rejected to avoid recurring vendor billing and third-party lock-in. Accepting self-hosted operations obligates the system to own WebSocket connection lifecycle, session capacity, and reconnect catch-up replay. See [`docs/decisions/change-delivery-mechanism.md`](decisions/change-delivery-mechanism.md).
